. But if they are going for Pungs, it's better to discard one of them.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the player can form a Pure Shifted Pungs. For example, three consecutive Pungs in the same suit. But they have B5, B6, and maybe B7. If they have Pungs of B5, B6, and B7, that's three consecutive numbers in Dots. That's worth 24 points. But they need three of each. They have two B5 and B6, so if they can get a third, that's possible.\n\nIn that case, keeping B5 and B6 is important. So discarding a B5 or B6 would hinder that. So better not to discard those.\n\nSo the best options are to discard W1, W2, T9, or maybe B8, B7, B9. Let's check those.\n\nT9 is a single tile. If they discard it, they lose a terminal. But if they are not going for All Terminals, it's okay.\n\nB8, B7, B9 are single tiles. If they discard one of them, they might be part of a sequence. For example, B7 is part of B5-B6-B7 (Chow). If they discard B7, they can't form that Chow. But if they have B5 and B6, they need a B7 for the Chow. So discarding B7 would prevent that.\n\nSo the best options are to discard W1, W2, or T9.\n\nBetween these, W1 and W2 are single tiles. T9 is a terminal. If the player is aiming for a hand that includes terminals, keeping T9 might be better. But since they don't have enough terminals, maybe it's better to discard T9.\n\nSo the best choice is to discard W2. Because W2 is a single tile not part of any Pung or potential Pung, and discarding it allows the player to focus on forming Pungs in T7, B5, B6, and F4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player has multiple pairs and potential for high-scoring patterns like All Pungs or Seven Pairs. Discarding a single tile that doesn't hinder these prospects is optimal.\n\nReason: Discarding W2 (a single tile not part of any Pung or potential Pung) prioritizes retaining pairs in T7, B5, B6, and F4 for high-value patterns like All Pungs (8 points). W2 is a middle tile that could be part of a Chow but is less critical than the existing pairs.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
