or W9 might be safe, as they are terminals. But if I discard them, I can't form a pair from them unless I draw another. But since there are many left, maybe it's okay.\n\nBut if I want Seven Pairs, I need to have seven pairs. Right now, I have four pairs. The remaining three pairs could be from B7, B8, B9, W1, W9, T9. Each needs to be drawn again. For example, if I can get another B7, B8, B9, then I can form pairs. Alternatively, maybe there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a hand with four pungs and a pair. But I don't have four pungs. I have B5x2, B6x2, T7x2, which are pairs, but not pungs. To form a pung, I need three of the same. So unless I can draw another B5, B6, or T7, I can't form pungs. The unshown tiles for B5 is 4, so there are 4 left. Since I have two, there are two more. Similarly for B6. So if I can draw one more B5 or B6, I can form a pung. But that would take time.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to focus on Seven Pairs. Let's see. If I discard one of the single tiles (B7, B8, B9, W1, W9, T9), then I can wait for another of those. For example, if I discard B7, and then draw another B7, I can form a pair. Similarly for others.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options are to Play F4, T9, B5, T7, W1, B8, B7, B9, W9, B6.\n\nI need to choose which tile to discard. Let's evaluate each option.\n\nPlay F4: I have two F4s. If I play one, I can keep the other as a pair. But since F4 is a pair, playing one might be a good move to keep the pair and wait for another F4. However, the unshown tiles for F4 is 4, so there are 4 left. Since I have two, there are two more. But playing one F4 would leave me with one F4 in hand. If I can get another F4, I can form a pair. But I already have a pair. Wait, no, I have two F4s. Playing one would leave me with one, but I need two for a pair. So playing F4 is not a good idea because I need to keep the pair.\n\nPlay T9: I have one T9. Discarding it would remove a terminal. If I don't need it for a pai