les. Let's see if they can form any scoring patterns. Since they have no melds, they need to form a concealed hand (Fully Concealed for 4 points). They have four chows and a pair? Let's check.\n\nLooking at the suited tiles: W1, W3, W5, W9; T4, T7x2, T9; B5, B6x2, B8. Maybe they can form chows. For example, T4 could be part of a T4-5-6 chow, but they have T4 and T7x2, T9. B5, B6x2, B8 could form B5-6-7 (if they have B7) but they don't. Maybe B6-7-8 (they have B6x2 and B8, but need B7). Not sure. The W1, W3, W5 might not form a chow. Similarly, W5 and W9 are isolated.\n\nAlternatively, looking for pairs. They have T7x2 and B6x2. So if they discard one of those, they can form a pair. For example, keeping B6 as a pair. Then the rest could form chows or pungs. But with the current tiles, it's not obvious.\n\nThey might be aiming for a Seven Pairs (24 points) or All Chows (2 points). But with the current tiles, it's not clear. Alternatively, maybe they can form a Mixed Straight (8 points) with chows in different suits.\n\nBut since the melds are empty, they need to form a concealed hand. Let's see. If they discard a tile that's not part of any potential chow or pung, maybe they can keep the hand flexible. For example, discarding J3 or J2 (dragons) might not help much. Alternatively, discarding a wind tile like W1, W3, W5, W9. Since they have four different winds, but not enough for any pungs.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, J3 has 2 left, which is low. If they discard J3, others might need it. But if they need it for a pair, maybe they should keep it. They have one J3 in hand. If they discard it, they can't form a pair of J3s. Similarly for J2.\n\nAlternatively, if they keep B6 as a pair, they might form All Chows. Let's see: B5, B6, B8. If they have B7, they could form B5-6-7 and B7-8-9. But they don't have B7. T4 could be part of T4-5-6, but they don't have T5. T7 and T9 could be part of T7-8-9, but they don't have T8. So maybe not.\n\nAnother angle: the next player h