w. So it's a single tile. But maybe safer than T4?\n\nHmm. Let's think about the current hand. If I discard T4, the remaining hand would be:\n\n[W1, W3, W5, W6, W9, T7, T8, F3, J2, J2] plus the existing melds. That leaves me with T7 and T8. If I can get T6 or T9, maybe form a Chow. But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W3, the hand would have W1, W5, W6, W9. Not helpful.\n\nBut perhaps T4 is better to discard. Also, looking at the melds of the other players, the previous player has a meld of B3, B4, B5 and B7, B8, B9. So they might be playing with B suits. Not sure if that affects my decision.\n\nIn terms of safety, T4 is a single tile with 4 left. So it's less likely to be needed by others. So maybe discarding T4 is the best option here.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard T4, as it is a single tile with multiple copies remaining and not part of any potential meld in the current hand.  \nReason: Discarding T4 reduces the risk of being claimed by others for a meld, while preserving key tiles like J2 for a potential Pung and maintaining flexibility for future melds.  \nAnswer: Play T4"}
